const markdown = "# SERVANA PRIVACY POLICY AND DATA PROTECTION NOTICE\n\n**Effective Date:** 01st January 2026\n**Last Updated:** 01st August 2026\n\n**Owner, Operator, Publisher, Contracting Entity, Data Controller and/or Data Processor, as applicable:**\n**Citrus Labs Limited**\n**P.O. Box 23983 - 00100, Nairobi, Kenya**\n**Email:** [support@citruslabs.co.ke](mailto:support@citruslabs.co.ke)\n\n---\n\n## 1. SCOPE, APPLICATION, AND LEGAL EFFECT\n\n1.1. This Servana Privacy Policy and Data Protection Notice (“**Policy**” or “**Notice**”) governs the collection, recording, organisation, structuring, storage, adaptation, alteration, retrieval, consultation, access, use, disclosure, transmission, dissemination, alignment, combination, restriction, erasure, destruction, retention, safeguarding, audit, validation, reconciliation, reporting, export, notification, investigation, and other Processing of Personal Data by Citrus Labs Limited in connection with Servana, including the Servana website, web application, SaaS dashboard, merchant account portal, finance dashboard, finance task inbox, invoice screens, payment-record screens, offline payment validation workflows, receipt workflows, partial and split payment workflows, dispute workflows, external refund records, cash-up and reconciliation screens, financial period screens, commission liability screens, platform fee screens, finance reports, finance exports, finance audit activity, customer support channels, APIs, emails, notifications, and related services.\n\n1.2. This Policy is specifically addressed to the **Merchant Finance** user category. A Merchant Finance account is a Customer-side, branch-scoped finance-control account used by an authorised finance officer, finance controller, accountant, cashier supervisor, reconciliation officer, branch finance reviewer, payment validator, or other authorised merchant-side user assigned to manage offline payment validation, invoice payment status, financial control, receipts, financial reports, reconciliation, disputes, external refund records, platform fee visibility where permitted, commission visibility where permitted, and finance audit activity within the Customer’s permitted merchant and branch scope.\n\n1.3. This Policy is not drafted for Super Administrators, Merchant Administrators, Merchant Branch account users, Merchant Human Resource account users, Merchant Front Office account users, Merchant Personnel account users, Merchant Audit account users, General End-User portal users, or service-recipient Clients as primary addressees. Those actors may be subject to separate, additional, role-specific, or context-specific notices. Where this Policy refers to such actors, it does so because a Merchant Finance user may view, validate, reject, review, dispute, export, reconcile, report on, or otherwise interact with records relating to those actors within the Merchant Finance user’s permitted branch scope and finance permissions.\n\n1.4. By accessing, registering for, logging into, using, validating, reviewing, exporting, reconciling, reporting on, administering, or otherwise interacting with Servana as a Merchant Finance user, the Merchant Finance user acknowledges that Personal Data will be Processed as described in this Policy, subject to Applicable Law, lawful exceptions, contractual terms, Customer responsibilities, Citrus Labs Limited’s data protection roles, and the privacy rights available to Data Subjects.\n\n1.5. This Policy is intended for publication and incorporation through Servana’s website, web application, SaaS dashboard, signup flow, subscription flow, account portal, finance dashboard, help centre, legal page, customer onboarding materials, and any other lawful electronic or written publication channel used by Citrus Labs Limited.\n\n---\n\n## 2. RELATIONSHIP WITH OTHER SERVANA LEGAL DOCUMENTS\n\n2.1. This Policy forms part of the legal and operational framework governing Servana and must be read together with Servana’s Terms and Conditions, SaaS subscription terms, customer agreements, order forms, data processing agreements, acceptable use policies, security policies, support policies, billing terms, finance access rules, role-based access disclosures, payment-validation rules, export-governance rules, and any written agreement between Citrus Labs Limited and the relevant Customer.\n\n2.2. This Policy does not replace any separate data processing agreement, customer agreement, employment agreement, contractor agreement, confidentiality agreement, acceptable use policy, subscription agreement, finance operations policy, cash-handling policy, staff policy, or internal Customer policy applicable to the Merchant Finance user. Where a signed written agreement contains more specific provisions governing a particular Processing activity, that agreement shall apply to the relevant Processing activity to the extent of the conflict, unless Applicable Law requires otherwise.\n\n2.3. This Policy is a privacy and data protection notice. It is not, by itself, a complete commercial contract for subscription fees, payment terms, service levels, warranties, indemnities, liability caps, intellectual property ownership, acceptable use, service availability, accounting standards, tax advice, refund policy, cash-handling procedures, or technical support, except where privacy-related provisions necessarily cross-reference those matters.\n\n---\n\n## 3. DEFINITIONS AND INTERPREATION\n\n3.1. In this Policy, unless the context requires otherwise:\n\n3.1.1. “**Account**” means a Servana account, merchant account, tenant account, user account, administrator account, finance account, branch account, staff account, internal account, or authenticated profile created, invited, authorised, suspended, restricted, maintained, or deleted in Servana.\n\n3.1.2. “**Administrator**” means a person or role with administrative permissions in Servana, including, depending on context, a Merchant Administrator, Super Administrator, Merchant Branch account user, Merchant Human Resource account user, Merchant Finance account user, or any other authorised administrator.\n\n3.1.3. “**Affiliate**” means any entity that directly or indirectly controls, is controlled by, or is under common control with Citrus Labs Limited, where such relationship exists.\n\n3.1.4. “**Applicable Law**” means all privacy, data protection, cybersecurity, consumer protection, electronic communications, e-commerce, records, tax, accounting, employment, evidentiary, regulatory, court, law enforcement, and other laws, regulations, binding directives, lawful guidance, orders, or rules applicable to Citrus Labs Limited, Servana, a Customer, a Data Subject, a Processing activity, or a relevant jurisdiction, including, where applicable, Kenya’s Data Protection Act, 2019, applicable Kenyan data protection regulations, lawful guidance of Kenya’s Office of the Data Protection Commissioner, African Union data protection principles, and privacy or data protection laws in other African jurisdictions where Servana may operate or where relevant Data Subjects are protected. Kenya’s ODPC identifies the Data Protection Act, 2019, the Data Protection (General) Regulations, 2021, the Data Protection (Registration of Data Controllers and Data Processors) Regulations, 2021, and the Data Protection (Complaints Handling and Enforcement Procedures) Regulations, 2021 among Kenya’s data protection legal instruments. ([ODPC][1])\n\n3.1.5. “**Branch**” or “**Merchant Branch**” means a physical or operational business location created by a Merchant Administrator within a merchant tenant, including, by way of example, a Westlands branch, Kilimani salon branch, CBD barbershop branch, hotel spa branch, massage parlour branch, grooming studio branch, beauty parlour branch, or similar operating unit.\n\n3.1.6. “**Citrus Labs Limited**”, “**Citrus**”, “**we**”, “**us**”, or “**our**” means Citrus Labs Limited, P.O. Box 23983 - 00100, Nairobi, Kenya.\n\n3.1.7. “**Client**” means a service recipient recorded in Servana by or on behalf of a merchant, including a person who receives, requests, books, waits for, is assigned to, pays for, is invoiced for, receives a receipt for, or is scheduled to receive services from a Customer’s Branch.\n\n3.1.8. “**Controller**” or “**Data Controller**” means the natural or legal person, public authority, agency, or other body which, alone or jointly with others, determines the purposes and means of Processing Personal Data.\n\n3.1.9. “**Customer**” means the merchant business, organisation, subscriber, account owner, business account, or other legal or commercial entity that registers for, subscribes to, configures, receives, or uses Servana for business operations.\n\n3.1.10. “**Customer Data**” means data, records, files, content, configurations, communications, instructions, Personal Data, business data, operational data, branch data, client records, staff records, service records, queue records, appointment records, service session records, invoices, receipts, offline payment records, platform fee records, commission records, reports, exports, audit logs, support records, and other information submitted to, generated in, stored in, or Processed through Servana by or on behalf of a Customer.\n\n3.1.11. “**Data Processor**” or “**Processor**” means a person or entity that Processes Personal Data on behalf of a Data Controller.\n\n3.1.12. “**Data Subject**” means an identified or identifiable natural person to whom Personal Data relates.\n\n3.1.13. “**Device Data**” means information relating to a browser, device, network, session, or technical environment, including IP address, user agent, browser type, operating system, device type, language setting, timezone, approximate location derived from IP address, session identifiers, cookie identifiers, diagnostic data, and related technical metadata.\n\n3.1.14. “**Finance Data**” means data relating to invoices, payment records, payment references, payment amounts, payment methods, payment dates, payment status, validation status, validation notes, rejection reasons, disputes, receipts, refunds, cash-up records, reconciliation records, commission liability records, platform fee records, finance reports, finance exports, financial-period locks, finance audit events, and related financial-control records.\n\n3.1.15. “**Merchant Administrator**” or “**Merchant Owner / Merchant Administrator**” means the Customer-side business owner, manager, authorised operator, subscriber, account owner, or primary merchant-side administrator who self-registers the merchant business account, after which the system creates the merchant tenant and assigns the registering user as Merchant Owner / Merchant Administrator.\n\n3.1.16. “**Merchant Finance**” or “**Merchant Finance Account**” means the Servana account type addressed by this Policy, assigned to a merchant and Branch scope and authorised to manage offline payment validation, financial control, invoice payment status, receipts, financial reports, reconciliation, disputes, external refund records, platform fee visibility where permitted, commission visibility where permitted, and finance audit activity, subject to role, tenant, branch, and permission controls.\n\n3.1.17. “**Personal Data**” or “**Personal Information**” means any information relating to an identified or identifiable natural person, whether directly or indirectly, and includes any equivalent concept under Applicable Law.\n\n3.1.18. “**Platform**”, “**Service**”, “**Servana**”, or “**Servana by Citrus**” means the multi-tenant SaaS web platform owned and operated by Citrus Labs Limited for service-based SMEs to manage merchant onboarding, merchant branches, staff access, client records, walk-ins, appointments, queues, service sessions, invoices, offline payment records, receipts, personnel commissions, platform fees, reports, notifications, audit logs, finance control, and related operational workflows.\n\n3.1.19. “**Processing**”, “**Process**”, or “**Processed**” means any operation or set of operations performed on Personal Data or on sets of Personal Data, whether or not by automated means.\n\n3.1.20. “**Sensitive Personal Data**” means Personal Data subject to heightened protection under Applicable Law, including, where applicable, health data, biometric data, genetic data, children’s data, precise location data, financial information requiring special protection, racial or ethnic origin, religious or philosophical beliefs, political opinions, trade union membership, sex life, sexual orientation, or any other category treated as sensitive, special-category, restricted, or protected under Applicable Law.\n\n3.1.21. “**Subprocessor**” means any third party appointed by Citrus Labs Limited to Process Personal Data on behalf of Citrus Labs Limited where Citrus Labs Limited acts as Processor.\n\n3.1.22. “**Super Administrator**” means the Citrus Labs Limited platform-owner role that governs Servana at platform level, including platform-wide settings, service fee rules, billing cycles, preferred-personnel waiting fees, platform reports, platform fee ledgers, suspicious usage monitoring, internal Citrus platform roles, feature flags, merchant suspension, merchant deactivation, billing enforcement, abuse response, and platform-policy enforcement.\n\n3.1.23. “**Usage Data**” means information relating to use of Servana, including pages viewed, dashboards accessed, features used, clicks, timestamps, login events, invoice views, payment-record views, validation actions, rejection actions, dispute actions, refund-record actions, receipt actions, finance report views, finance export requests, cash-up reviews, commission liability views, platform fee views, financial-period actions, finance audit views, support activity, API requests, session duration, error events, security events, and operational telemetry.\n\n3.1.24. “**User**” means any individual who accesses or uses Servana, including a Merchant Finance user, Merchant Administrator, Super Administrator, Merchant Branch account user, Merchant Human Resource account user, Merchant Front Office account user, Merchant Personnel account user, Merchant Audit account user, invited user, support user, or other authorised account user.\n\n3.2. References to “including” mean “including without limitation”. References to “law” include statutes, regulations, binding guidance, lawful orders, and competent authority decisions. Headings are for convenience only and do not limit interpretation. Singular terms include plural terms and plural terms include singular terms where context permits.\n\n---\n\n## 4. DESCRIPTION OF SERVANA\n\n4.1. Servana is a multi-tenant SaaS web platform for service-based SMEs, including barbershops, salons, massage parlours, spas, grooming studios, beauty parlours, and similar appointment-based or walk-in businesses.\n\n4.2. Servana is designed to operate as a service-operations platform through which merchant businesses can digitise, control, and audit day-to-day operations, including merchant onboarding, merchant branches, staff and personnel access, front-office operations, client records, walk-ins, appointments, queue management, service delivery tracking, client choice of next available personnel or preferred personnel, invoice generation, offline payment recording, receipt generation, personnel commission visibility, client data protection, contact access control, platform fee calculation, service fee tier invoice pricing, merchant financial visibility, and audit-ready operational and financial records. \n\n4.3. Servana must not be treated solely as a booking application, point-of-sale system, payment processor, invoicing tool, staff roster, or reporting tool. It is a service-operations SaaS platform intended to help Customers run clients, staff, services, queues, invoices, commissions, platform fees, reports, and audit records from one secure web dashboard.\n\n4.4. Servana is structured around tenant isolation. Each merchant operates as an isolated tenant. A merchant’s data must not be accessible, inferable, editable, exportable, or enumerable by another merchant, subject only to controlled Citrus Labs Limited platform-level workflows, lawful support access, security operations, legal compliance, billing enforcement, abuse controls, and other lawful exceptions.\n\n4.5. Servana records offline or off-platform payments. Servana may record payment method, amount, reference, status, validation details, payment notes, partial payment records, split payment records, external refund records, cash-up records, reconciliation records, disputes, receipts, and related audit trails, but Servana does not, by this Policy alone, represent that it directly processes client-to-merchant payments inside the platform.\n\n4.6. Servana includes finance-control functions that permit the Merchant Finance user, within the assigned Branch scope and permissions, to view invoices, validate offline payments, approve or reject payment records, record and audit payment references, generate or trigger receipts after payment validation, view outstanding balances, view voided invoices, view externally recorded refunds, view platform fees where permitted, view commission liabilities where permitted, export finance reports where permitted, review payment disputes, audit payment activity, review cash-up submissions, approve or reject branch cash-ups where permitted, and manage finance task inbox items.\n\n---\n\n## 5. MERCHANT FINANCE ROLE AND PRIVACY TREATMENT\n\n5.1. The Merchant Finance account is a Customer-side finance-control account within Servana. Its purpose is to manage offline payment validation, financial control, invoice payment status, receipts, financial reports, reconciliation, disputes, external refund records, platform fee visibility where permitted, commission visibility where permitted, and finance audit activity.\n\n5.2. Merchant Finance users must not access other branches externally or within the same Merchant Administrator except for the Merchant Branch into which the Merchant Finance user has been assigned, unless Servana, the Customer, and Applicable Law expressly permit a broader scope.\n\n5.3. Merchant Finance must not launch or operate as one broad unrestricted finance permission. Finance access must be granular, permission-controlled, branch-scoped, tenant-scoped, auditable, and proportionate to the finance user’s lawful role.\n\n5.4. A Merchant Finance user may, where permitted, view invoices within assigned merchant and branch scope, validate offline payments, approve or reject payment records, edit payment references under controlled and audited workflows, generate receipts only after validation rules are satisfied, void finance records under restricted and approval-based workflows, review disputes, export finance reports in CSV or PDF where permitted, lock periods only where delegated or permitted, view commissions where permitted, and view platform fees where permitted.\n\n5.5. Merchant Finance users may Process or view payment validation statuses, including pending validation, validated, rejected, partially validated, disputed, correction requested, voided, and refunded externally.\n\n5.6. Merchant Finance users may Process or view payment validation fields, including invoice identifier, payment record identifier, payment method, payment amount, payment reference, recorded-by user, validated-by user, validation status, validation note, rejection reason, validation timestamp, branch identifier, and merchant identifier.\n\n5.7. Merchant Finance users may Process or view method-specific payment reference information, including cash amount, collector information, branch or day cash-up reference, M-Pesa transaction code or reference, bank reference, deposit slip reference, card terminal reference, voucher code, voucher approval status, split-payment leg details, and other merchant-defined references where applicable.\n\n5.8. Merchant Finance users may Process or view duplicate payment-reference alerts, same-merchant conflicts, same-branch conflicts, override workflows, override reasons, and override audit logs.\n\n5.9. Merchant Finance users may Process or view partial payments and split payments, including payment-leg statuses, remaining balances, multiple payment records for a single invoice, mixed payment methods, receipt rules based on validated payment amounts, and final paid status only when validated totals equal invoice totals.\n\n5.10. Merchant Finance users may Process or view receipt controls, including receipt generation locks, unique receipt numbering, receipt reversal, receipt cancellation, receipt reissue references, receipt download logs, receipt permissions, and receipt audit logs.\n\n5.11. Merchant Finance users may Process or view invoice adjustment and void approval workflows, including void unpaid invoice actions, void paid invoice approvals, invoice adjustments after payment, preferred-personnel fee reversals, external refund approvals, and restricted reopening of paid invoices.\n\n5.12. Merchant Finance users may Process or view daily branch cash-up and reconciliation workflows, including daily invoices, recorded payments, pending validations, expected totals, recorded totals, payment method breakdowns, cash counted, M-Pesa references, bank references, card references, discrepancy flags, branch cash-up submissions, finance approvals, finance rejections, and period lock status.\n\n5.13. Merchant Finance users may Process or view dispute records, including dispute type, invoice identifier, payment record identifier, raised-by user, assigned-to user, amount in dispute, reason, evidence attachment, resolution note, resolved-by user, and resolved-at timestamp.\n\n5.14. Merchant Finance users may Process or view external refund records, including refund type, refund amount, refund method, refund reference, approval workflow, invoice impact, payment impact, and refund audit logs.\n\n5.15. Merchant Finance users may Process or view commission liability records where permitted, including earned commissions, pending commissions, reversals on voided invoices, adjustments on refunded invoices, commission dispute flags, commission exports, and period-based commission summaries.\n\n5.16. Merchant Finance users may Process or view platform fee visibility and reconciliation records where permitted, including platform fee ledgers, charges, payments, adjustments, outstanding balances, fee calculation explanations, billing cycles, due dates, overdue status, preferred-personnel fee treatment, platform fee disputes, and merchant-facing platform fee statements.\n\n5.17. Because the Merchant Finance role is financially sensitive, the Merchant Finance user’s actions may be extensively logged, including invoice views, payment-record views, validation decisions, rejection decisions, payment reference edits, duplicate-reference overrides, dispute actions, refund actions, receipt generation, receipt reissue, finance exports, cash-up reviews, period locks, period reopenings, platform fee views, commission views, IP address, user agent, timestamp, target record, branch identifier, merchant identifier, severity, and related audit metadata.\n\n---\n\n## 6. CITRUS LABS LIMITED’S DATA PROTECTION ROLES\n\n6.1. Citrus Labs Limited may act as an independent Data Controller where it determines the purposes and means of Processing, including Processing relating to account administration, authentication, product telemetry, security monitoring, fraud prevention, abuse controls, platform-policy enforcement, support operations, analytics, service improvement, direct communications, legal compliance, regulatory cooperation, legal claims, and Citrus business administration.\n\n6.2. Citrus Labs Limited may act as a Data Processor where it Processes Customer Data on behalf of a Customer that determines the relevant purposes and means of Processing, including Client records, merchant staff records, HR records, Branch records, appointment records, walk-in records, queue records, service session records, invoices, receipts, offline payment records, payment validation records, commission records, disputes, external refund records, cash-up records, reconciliation records, finance reports, exports, audit logs, and Customer-configured workflows.\n\n6.3. Citrus Labs Limited may act as a service provider, operator, SaaS platform provider, joint controller, independent controller, processor, subprocessor appointor, or other legally recognised role depending on the specific Processing activity, Applicable Law, contractual arrangements, and factual context.\n\n6.4. Where Citrus Labs Limited acts as Processor, Citrus Labs Limited Processes Personal Data on documented Customer instructions, subject to Applicable Law, lawful exceptions, security obligations, support requirements, data processing agreements, and the need to preserve platform integrity.\n\n6.5. Where Citrus Labs Limited acts as Controller, Citrus Labs Limited may determine lawful purposes, lawful bases, retention periods, security measures, disclosures, analytics, compliance responses, and enforcement actions in accordance with this Policy and Applicable Law.\n\n---\n\n## 7. CUSTOMER-CONTROLLED DATA AND CITRUS-CONTROLLED DATA\n\n7.1. Customer-controlled data may include merchant profile data, business category data, merchant logo data, Branch profile data, Branch addresses, Branch contact details, Branch operating calendars, Branch status records, staff records, HR records, employment status, personnel service eligibility, availability calendars, Client profiles, Client contact details, visit history, service history, appointment history, queue history, assigned personnel history, preferred personnel history, service notes, invoices, receipt records, payment records, payment references, split payment records, partial payment records, external refund records, disputes, commission rules, commission liabilities, cash-up records, reconciliation records, consent records, communication preferences, finance reports, finance exports, Branch reports, Branch audit logs, finance audit logs, and Customer audit review records.\n\n7.2. Citrus-controlled data may include Merchant Finance account metadata, authentication logs, Magic Link token metadata, platform usage telemetry, error logs, security logs, abuse monitoring records, suspicious activity records, support records, Citrus legal records, product analytics, system performance data, audit infrastructure metadata, and internal Citrus operational records.\n\n7.3. Certain data may be mixed-context data. For example, a finance audit log may contain a Merchant Finance actor identifier, merchant tenant identifier, Branch identifier, action, target entity, old values, new values, severity, event status, IP address, user agent, record hash, previous record hash, and timestamp. Citrus Labs Limited may control the audit system for platform integrity, while the underlying invoice, payment, receipt, dispute, refund, commission, cash-up, reconciliation, Client, staff, or Branch record may be Customer-controlled data.\n\n7.4. Citrus Labs Limited may retain rights in non-personal platform metadata, aggregated information, anonymised information, de-identified information, product telemetry, security insights, service-improvement insights, and statistical data, provided such Processing is conducted in accordance with Applicable Law.\n\n---\n\n## 8. CATEGORIES OF PERSONAL DATA PROCESSED\n\n8.1. Citrus Labs Limited may Process the following Personal Data relating to the Merchant Finance user:\n\n8.1.1. **Identity Data**, including name, display name, role, account identifier, merchant tenant association, Branch assignment, finance role status, and profile information.\n\n8.1.2. **Contact Data**, including email address, phone number where submitted or required by Customer policy, business contact details, support contact details, notification address, and communication identifiers.\n\n8.1.3. **Authentication Data**, including Magic Link token metadata, token issue time, token expiry time, token status, email verification status, login timestamps, failed login attempts, account status, role status, suspension status, Branch-scope metadata, session identifiers, rate-limit events, optional MFA status where enabled, and security challenge metadata.\n\n8.1.4. **Organisation, Tenant, and Branch Data**, including Customer identity, merchant identifier, Branch identifier, Branch name, Branch code, assigned Branch scope, finance dashboard visibility, report scope, invoice visibility scope, receipt visibility scope, payment-record visibility scope, and finance permission scope.\n\n8.1.5. **Role and Permission Data**, including invoice view permission, payment validation permission, payment reference edit permission, receipt generation permission, finance record void permission, dispute review permission, finance export permission, financial period lock permission, commission view permission, platform fee view permission, and role history.\n\n8.1.6. **Finance Activity Data**, including invoice views, payment-record views, validation decisions, rejection decisions, partial validation actions, correction requests, disputed-status actions, void actions, external refund actions, receipt generation, receipt reissue, receipt download records, duplicate-reference override activity, finance task inbox activity, and approval workflow participation.\n\n8.1.7. **Payment Validation Data**, including invoice identifier, payment record identifier, payment method, payment amount, payment reference, recorded-by user, validated-by user, validation status, validation note, rejection reason, validation timestamp, Branch identifier, merchant identifier, duplicate-reference state, payment leg details, and split-payment details.\n\n8.1.8. **Dispute and Evidence Data**, including dispute type, invoice identifier, payment record identifier, raised-by user, assigned-to user, amount in dispute, reason, evidence attachment, resolution note, resolved-by user, resolved-at timestamp, dispute status, and escalation status.\n\n8.1.9. **Refund Data**, including refund type, refund amount, refund method, refund reference, approval workflow records, invoice impact, payment impact, created-by user, approved-by user, and audit log data.\n\n8.1.10. **Cash-Up and Reconciliation Data**, including expected revenue, validated payments, pending validation totals, rejected payment totals, cash totals, M-Pesa totals, bank transfer totals, card terminal totals, voucher totals, split payment totals, discrepancy amounts, cash counted, references, notes, review status, approval status, rejection status, and period lock status.\n\n8.1.11. **Commission and Platform Fee Visibility Data**, including commission payable lists, pending commissions, earned commissions, reversal records, commission dispute flags, commission exports, platform fee ledgers, platform fee charges, platform fee payments, platform fee adjustments, outstanding balances, billing cycles, overdue status, and platform fee dispute records where permitted.\n\n8.1.12. **Support and Communication Data**, including support requests, messages, attachments, screenshots, diagnostics, troubleshooting notes, feedback, complaints, administrative communications, and service correspondence.\n\n8.1.13. **Usage Data**, including finance dashboard access, invoice views, payment-record views, validation actions, report views, export requests, download activity, receipt activity, dispute activity, refund activity, cash-up review activity, period lock activity, platform fee views, commission views, finance audit activity, and account configuration activity.\n\n8.1.14. **Device, Log, and Technical Data**, including IP address, user agent, browser type, device type, operating system, timezone, approximate location derived from IP address, API metadata, session logs, error logs, diagnostic logs, performance metadata, and security metadata.\n\n8.1.15. **Security and Audit Data**, including actor identifiers, actor role, merchant identifier, Branch identifier, action, target entity type, target entity identifier, old values, new values, severity, event status, IP address, user agent, record hash, previous record hash, unauthorised access attempts, suspicious activity flags, and timestamps.\n\n8.1.16. **Preference Data**, including notification preferences, dashboard preferences, theme preferences, table preferences, saved finance filters where enabled, report preferences, and interface settings.\n\n8.1.17. **Marketing, Relationship, and Feedback Data**, including lawful marketing preferences, survey responses, feedback, business relationship notes, product interest records, and opt-out records.\n\n8.2. Citrus Labs Limited may Process the following Personal Data relating to other actors where the Merchant Finance user is permitted to view or interact with Branch-scoped or finance-scoped records:\n\n8.2.1. **Merchant Administrator data**, including Merchant Administrator approvals, financial period lock authority, platform fee visibility, platform fee disputes, merchant service fee tier records, Branch debt records, and merchant-level finance oversight records.\n\n8.2.2. **Merchant Branch account user data**, including Branch day opening records, Branch day closing records, Branch day reopening records, cash-up submissions, Branch payment summaries, Branch discrepancy notes, invoice visibility, receipt visibility, payment-record visibility, and Branch reports.\n\n8.2.3. **Merchant Human Resource account user data**, including commission rule records, personnel service eligibility records where relevant to commission or service delivery, staff profile references, role references, and commission-related staff records.\n\n8.2.4. **Merchant Front Office account user data**, including invoice creation, offline payment recording, payment method recording, payment reference submission, awaiting-finance-validation status, ready-for-receipt status, Client check-in records, service session records, and payment-related front-office actions.\n\n8.2.5. **Merchant Personnel account user data**, including assigned personnel, services performed, commission records, commission liability records, preferred-personnel requests, preferred-personnel surcharge records, and service-completion records.\n\n8.2.6. **Merchant Audit account user data**, including finance audit reviews, high-risk event reviews, payment issue reviews, role change reviews, receipt review records, finance export review records, and flagged event records where permitted.\n\n8.2.7. **Client record data**, including Client profile, phone number, optional email, optional gender, visit history, service history, assigned personnel history, preferred personnel history, invoice history, receipt history, appointment history, queue participation, payment history, payment method references, Client preferences, consent records, communication preferences, notes, duplicate-client prevention records, and optional future portal records where enabled.\n\n---\n\n## 9. SENSITIVE PERSONAL DATA AND SPECIAL-CATEGORY DATA\n\n9.1. Servana does not require Merchant Finance users to submit Sensitive Personal Data for ordinary finance account access, routine payment validation, invoice review, receipt control, cash-up review, or reconciliation.\n\n9.2. Sensitive Personal Data may nevertheless be Processed where the Customer, Merchant Administrator, Branch user, HR user, Front Office user, Finance user, Personnel user, Client, support requester, or other authorised person submits it into Servana, including through client notes, service notes, staff records, employment records, dispute evidence, refund evidence, payment evidence, support attachments, screenshots, communication records, consent records, or audit records.\n\n9.3. The Customer and Merchant Finance user must not submit or cause the submission of Sensitive Personal Data unless there is a lawful basis, appropriate notice, required consent or authorisation where applicable, appropriate role-based access configuration, and compliance with Applicable Law.\n\n9.4. Where Citrus Labs Limited Processes Sensitive Personal Data, it will do so subject to Applicable Law, lawful basis requirements, access restrictions, masking, retention controls, security safeguards, contractual obligations, and lawful Customer instructions where Citrus Labs Limited acts as Processor.\n\n9.5. Merchant Finance users may see limited Sensitive Personal Data where necessary for lawful payment validation, dispute review, refund review, cash-up review, commission review, platform fee dispute review, legal preservation, audit investigation, or support. Full visibility must not be assumed and may be restricted by permission, Branch scope, Customer configuration, lawful basis, masking controls, and Applicable Law.\n\n---\n\n## 10. CHILDREN’S AND MINORS’ DATA\n\n10.1. Servana is intended for business use by merchants and authorised business users. It is not designed for independent use by children.\n\n10.2. Customers may record minors as service-recipient Clients where lawful and relevant to the Customer’s business operations. Such records may include client profile details, appointment history, queue participation, service history, invoice history, receipt history, payment history, consent records, communication preferences, or guardian-related information where lawfully submitted.\n\n10.3. The Customer and Merchant Administrator are responsible for obtaining parental or guardian consent where required, issuing appropriate notices, limiting access, ensuring lawful Processing, and complying with child-protection and data protection requirements applicable to minors’ data.\n\n10.4. Merchant Finance users must treat minors’ data, children’s invoice records, payment records, receipt records, refund records, dispute records, cash-up records, and any finance record involving minors as restricted information and must not access, export, copy, disclose, or use such data except as authorised and necessary for lawful finance operations.\n\n10.5. Citrus Labs Limited may restrict, delete, preserve, or refer minors’ data to the relevant Customer where required or permitted by Applicable Law, contractual obligations, or lawful Customer instructions.\n\n---\n\n## 11. SOURCES OF PERSONAL DATA\n\n11.1. Citrus Labs Limited may collect Personal Data directly from the Merchant Finance user during account invitation, activation, authentication, finance dashboard use, invoice review, payment validation, payment rejection, payment reference editing, receipt generation, receipt reissue, dispute review, external refund recording, cash-up review, reconciliation review, finance export activity, support requests, and platform use.\n\n11.2. Citrus Labs Limited may collect Personal Data from Customers, Merchant Administrators, Merchant Branch account users, Merchant Human Resource account users, Merchant Front Office account users, Merchant Personnel account users, Merchant Audit account users, Clients, invited users, authorised representatives, support contacts, and records generated through Servana workflows.\n\n11.3. Citrus Labs Limited may collect Personal Data automatically from browsers, devices, networks, APIs, cookies, local storage, session storage, security systems, logs, monitoring systems, analytics technologies, infrastructure systems, error tracking systems, audit systems, and product telemetry.\n\n11.4. Citrus Labs Limited may collect Personal Data from integrations, email delivery services, SMS or WhatsApp services where enabled, billing processes, payment-related processes where applicable, third-party services, public sources where lawful, professional advisers, regulators, courts, law enforcement agencies, and other lawful sources.\n\n---\n\n## 12. PURPOSES OF PROCESSING\n\n12.1. Citrus Labs Limited may Process Personal Data for the following purposes:\n\n12.1.1. creating, inviting, activating, authenticating, administering, suspending, restricting, or deleting Merchant Finance accounts;\n\n12.1.2. enabling Magic Link login, one-time-use tokens, token expiry, email verification, tenant verification, role verification, account status verification, suspension checks, Branch-scope verification, session management, login rate limiting, login audit logs, and optional MFA where enabled;\n\n12.1.3. enabling Merchant Finance users to access the assigned finance dashboard and manage offline payment validation, invoice payment status, financial control, receipts, finance reports, reconciliation, disputes, external refund records, platform fee visibility where permitted, commission visibility where permitted, and finance audit activity;\n\n12.1.4. enabling invoice review, invoice payment status tracking, unpaid invoice visibility, paid invoice visibility, partially paid invoice visibility, voided invoice visibility, outstanding balance visibility, invoice adjustment review, invoice void workflow participation, and invoice-related audit logs;\n\n12.1.5. enabling offline payment validation, including payment method review, payment amount review, payment reference review, payment date and time review, payment note review, recorded-by user review, validation status changes, validation notes, rejection reasons, correction requests, partial validation, dispute marking, voiding, and external refund status;\n\n12.1.6. enabling method-specific payment reference controls for cash, M-Pesa, bank transfer, card terminal, voucher, split payment, and other merchant-defined offline payment methods;\n\n12.1.7. enabling duplicate payment-reference detection, same-merchant conflict detection, same-branch conflict detection, override workflows, override permissions, override reasons, and override audit logging;\n\n12.1.8. enabling partial payment and split payment control, including remaining balance calculation, multiple payment records per invoice, mixed-method payment records, payment-leg validation, receipt rules based on validated amounts, and final paid status only when validated totals equal invoice totals;\n\n12.1.9. enabling receipt controls, including receipt generation only after validation rules are satisfied, unique receipt numbering, receipt reversal without deletion, receipt reissue references, receipt download logs, and receipt permissions;\n\n12.1.10. enabling dispute management, including open, under review, evidence requested, resolved, rejected, escalated, and closed dispute statuses, dispute type, invoice identifier, payment record identifier, raised-by user, assigned-to user, amount in dispute, reason, evidence attachment, resolution note, resolved-by user, and resolved-at timestamp;\n\n12.1.11. enabling external refund recording, including full or partial refund type, refund amount, refund method, refund reference, approval workflow, invoice impact, payment impact, and refund audit logs;\n\n12.1.12. enabling daily Branch cash-up and reconciliation workflows, including daily invoice review, recorded payment review, pending validation review, expected totals, recorded totals, payment method breakdowns, cash counted, M-Pesa references, bank references, card references, discrepancy flags, Branch cash-up submissions, finance approval, finance rejection, and post-approval period locking;\n\n12.1.13. enabling financial period controls, including daily locks, monthly locks, lock permissions, post-lock edit workflows, financial period reopening, reasons, approvals, immutable audit logs, and reporting protection;\n\n12.1.14. enabling commission liability review where permitted, including commission payable lists, earned commissions, pending commissions, payment dependency, reversals, refunds, commission disputes, commission exports, and period-based summaries;\n\n12.1.15. enabling platform fee visibility and reconciliation where permitted, including platform fee ledger records, charges, payments, adjustments, outstanding balances, calculation explanations, billing cycles, due dates, overdue status, preferred personnel fee treatment, platform fee disputes, and merchant-facing statements;\n\n12.1.16. enabling finance export governance, including export permission, export reason, export scope, Branch scope, date range, report type, signed URL expiry, download count, audit logs, and sensitive data masking;\n\n12.1.17. enabling finance notifications and task inbox items, including new payment pending validation, duplicate payment reference detected, paid invoice awaiting receipt, payment dispute opened, refund approval requested, invoice void approval requested, cash-up submitted for review, Branch cash-up discrepancy, commission liability generated, platform fee overdue, and financial period ready for lock;\n\n12.1.18. protecting client data through contact access restrictions, no Merchant Personnel bulk contact export, sensitive client contact and payment data masking, unauthorised contact-access attempt logging, consent record tracking, signed URLs for permitted exports, and contact-access or legally permitted export audit logs;\n\n12.1.19. monitoring, detecting, preventing, investigating, and responding to unauthorised access, tenant isolation violations, Branch isolation violations, fraud, abuse, duplicate payment references, fake payment records, payment manipulation, receipt manipulation, finance export misuse, suspicious usage, security incidents, policy violations, platform misuse, and unlawful activity;\n\n12.1.20. maintaining audit logs for payment recorded, payment edited, payment validated, payment rejected, payment disputed, payment voided, payment reference overridden, receipt generated, receipt reissued, invoice voided, refund recorded externally, financial period locked, financial period reopened, finance export generated, cash-up reviewed, discrepancy flagged, platform fee viewed, commission liability reviewed, and unauthorised access attempted;\n\n12.1.21. providing support, troubleshooting, diagnostics, bug fixing, remote assistance where authorised, account recovery, service restoration, incident response, and operational continuity;\n\n12.1.22. performing analytics, diagnostics, product telemetry, service improvement, performance monitoring, error tracking, capacity planning, product roadmap planning, security hardening, fraud prevention, and service reliability analysis;\n\n12.1.23. complying with Applicable Law, legal obligations, tax and accounting duties, regulatory requests, court orders, subpoenas, law enforcement requests, data protection obligations, breach response obligations, evidentiary preservation duties, and audit obligations;\n\n12.1.24. enforcing terms, customer agreements, acceptable use obligations, billing obligations, platform policies, legal rights, claims, defences, remedies, and security controls;\n\n12.1.25. supporting backups, disaster recovery, business continuity, redundancy, archival copies, scheduled jobs, queue processing, background reports, imports, exports, emails, notifications, and production monitoring;\n\n12.1.26. conducting corporate transactions, including merger, acquisition, financing, restructuring, insolvency, due diligence, asset sale, transfer, or similar business events;\n\n12.1.27. sending direct marketing, product updates, surveys, relationship communications, and lawful commercial communications where permitted by Applicable Law.\n\n---\n\n## 13. LAWFUL BASES FOR PROCESSING\n\n13.1. Citrus Labs Limited relies on one or more lawful bases depending on the Processing context, jurisdiction, role, Personal Data category, and relationship between Citrus Labs Limited, the Customer, the Merchant Finance user, and the Data Subject.\n\n13.2. Citrus Labs Limited may Process Personal Data where Processing is necessary for performance of a contract or to take steps relating to a contract, including finance account administration, SaaS delivery, platform operation, authentication, support, security, finance dashboard operation, invoice review, payment validation, receipt control, cash-up review, finance reports, finance exports, and Customer administration.\n\n13.3. Citrus Labs Limited may Process Personal Data where Processing is necessary for legitimate interests pursued by Citrus Labs Limited, the Customer, or another lawful party, provided such interests are not overridden by the rights and freedoms of the Data Subject under Applicable Law. Legitimate interests may include platform security, tenant isolation, Branch isolation, fraud prevention, abuse prevention, auditability, payment integrity, receipt integrity, cash-up reconciliation, commission dispute prevention, platform fee dispute prevention, access control, service improvement, analytics, billing enforcement, dispute resolution, customer support, product development, internal accountability, legal defence, and commercial administration.\n\n13.4. Citrus Labs Limited may Process Personal Data where the Data Subject has given consent, including for optional communications, optional cookies, optional profile fields, optional future features, certain marketing communications, or Processing of certain Sensitive Personal Data where consent is required by Applicable Law.\n\n13.5. Citrus Labs Limited may Process Personal Data where Processing is necessary for compliance with legal obligations, including data protection obligations, cybersecurity obligations, tax obligations, accounting obligations, regulatory cooperation, court process, law enforcement cooperation, breach notification, audit obligations, and legally required retention.\n\n13.6. Citrus Labs Limited may Process Personal Data where Processing is necessary to protect vital interests, respond to emergencies, prevent serious harm, or protect life or safety, where applicable.\n\n13.7. Citrus Labs Limited may Process Personal Data where Processing is necessary for a task carried out in the public interest or under lawful authority, where such basis applies.\n\n13.8. Where Citrus Labs Limited acts as Processor, Citrus Labs Limited Processes Personal Data on documented Customer instructions, subject to Applicable Law, lawful exceptions, and the relevant data processing agreement.\n\n---\n\n## 14. CONSENT\n\n14.1. Where consent is used as the lawful basis for Processing, Citrus Labs Limited may obtain, document, and manage consent through account flows, checkboxes, settings, consent banners, cookie tools, written confirmations, electronic acceptance, role-based approvals, Customer-configured consent records, or other lawful methods.\n\n14.2. Consent may be withdrawn where Applicable Law permits withdrawal. Withdrawal does not affect Processing conducted before withdrawal and does not prevent continued Processing where another lawful basis applies, including contractual necessity, legal obligation, legitimate interests, security, legal claims, financial integrity, receipt integrity, audit preservation, retention obligations, or Customer instructions.\n\n14.3. Where a Customer is responsible for obtaining consent from its Clients, staff, personnel, contractors, employees, Branch users, finance users, front-office users, audit users, or other Data Subjects, the Customer and Merchant Administrator remain responsible for the validity, scope, content, recordkeeping, and lawfulness of that consent.\n\n14.4. Citrus Labs Limited may refuse to act on an attempted withdrawal of consent where Processing is required or permitted by law, required for security, required to preserve audit logs, necessary to perform a contract, necessary to maintain financial records, necessary to preserve invoices or receipts, necessary to comply with legal obligations, or necessary to defend legal claims.\n\n---\n\n## 15. LEGITIMATE INTERESTS\n\n15.1. Citrus Labs Limited’s legitimate interests include operating Servana as a reliable multi-tenant SaaS platform, protecting merchant tenants against cross-tenant exposure, protecting Branch-scoped data against cross-Branch exposure, preventing unauthorised access, preventing fraud, enforcing access controls, preserving audit trails, maintaining tamper-evident records, supporting Customer finance operations, detecting suspicious activity, detecting duplicate payment references, preventing fake or duplicated payment records, preserving invoice and receipt integrity, providing support, improving service quality, maintaining business continuity, enforcing rights, billing Customers, managing commercial operations, and protecting Citrus Labs Limited, Customers, Users, Clients, and third parties.\n\n15.2. Citrus Labs Limited may conduct legitimate-interest assessments where required by Applicable Law and may apply safeguards, including data minimisation, role-based access, Branch-based access, masking, logging, retention limits, opt-outs where applicable, and objection handling.\n\n---\n\n## 16. ACCOUNT REGISTRATION AND AUTHENTICATION DISCLOSURES\n\n16.1. Merchant Finance users may be invited, added, assigned, activated, suspended, deactivated, removed, or restricted according to the Customer’s permitted role-management workflows, Merchant Administrator authority, Merchant Human Resource branch-scoped staff-management authority where applicable, Citrus Labs Limited platform controls, and Applicable Law.\n\n16.2. Merchant Finance login is conducted by Magic Link sent to email. A user must not log in merely because the email address exists. Servana may verify that the email exists, the user belongs to the correct merchant tenant, the account is active, the role is active, the user has not been suspended, the Branch scope is valid where applicable, and the Magic Link is valid, unused, and unexpired.\n\n16.3. Citrus Labs Limited may Process login events, failed login attempts, Magic Link requests, token metadata, IP addresses, user agents, account status, suspension status, Branch-scope metadata, session status, and security events.\n\n16.4. Citrus Labs Limited may rate-limit registration, login, Magic Link, invitation acceptance, account settings, API, export, finance actions, validation actions, receipt actions, download, and sensitive endpoints to protect Servana against brute force, credential stuffing, scraping, excessive access, abuse, payment manipulation, and unauthorised access.\n\n---\n\n## 17. CUSTOMER ORGANISATION, ADMINISTRATOR, AND TENANT ACCOUNT DISCLOSURES\n\n17.1. The Merchant Finance user acts under the Customer’s authority and within the Customer’s merchant tenant, subject to role limitations, Branch scope, finance permission scope, server-side access controls, audit visibility restrictions, export governance, data masking, and Applicable Law.\n\n17.2. The Merchant Administrator or other authorised Customer-side administrator may create, invite, restrict, suspend, deactivate, remove, monitor, or audit the Merchant Finance account where permitted by Servana’s role model, Customer configuration, customer agreement, and Applicable Law.\n\n17.3. The Merchant Finance user may be visible to Merchant Administrators, Super Administrators, Merchant Audit users, authorised support personnel, and other authorised users through account records, role records, staff records, login logs, audit logs, report logs, operational records, Branch records, finance records, and security records.\n\n17.4. The Merchant Finance user must understand that finance access may expose Personal Data belonging to Clients, staff, personnel, Front Office users, Branch users, HR users, Merchant Administrators, and other Data Subjects. Such access is granted solely for lawful finance control, payment validation, invoice status review, receipt control, cash-up review, reconciliation, dispute management, external refund recording, commission review where permitted, platform fee review where permitted, finance reporting, finance export, and accountability purposes.\n\n17.5. Citrus Labs Limited may log administrator and finance actions, including before-and-after values for sensitive changes, actor identifiers, role, Branch, merchant, IP address, user agent, timestamp, severity, and event status.\n\n---\n\n## 18. USER-GENERATED CONTENT, UPLOADED DATA, AND SERVANA RECORDS\n\n18.1. Servana may Process user-generated content, uploaded content, notes, attachments, screenshots, evidence, service notes, dispute materials, refund materials, Branch records, staff records, client records, merchant logos, invoices, receipts, payment records, reports, exports, audit records, and other Customer Data.\n\n18.2. The Merchant Finance user may view, create, edit, submit, validate, reject, export, or manage user-generated content or uploaded data only where such activity is permitted by role, Branch scope, Customer configuration, finance purpose, and Applicable Law.\n\n18.3. The Customer and Merchant Finance user must ensure that validation notes, rejection reasons, correction requests, dispute reasons, evidence attachments, refund reasons, cash-up discrepancy notes, finance export reasons, reopening reasons, support notes, and report notes are accurate, lawful, necessary, proportionate, professional, and do not include unnecessary Sensitive Personal Data.\n\n18.4. Citrus Labs Limited may restrict, quarantine, preserve, delete, review, or disclose uploaded content where necessary for security, support, legal compliance, abuse prevention, incident response, rights enforcement, or lawful Customer instructions.\n\n---\n\n## 19. COMMUNICATIONS, NOTIFICATIONS, AND MARKETING\n\n19.1. Citrus Labs Limited may send transactional, security, operational, administrative, support, billing, platform, finance, and legal communications relating to Servana.\n\n19.2. Servana may send Magic Link login emails, staff welcome emails, staff activation emails, staff invitation emails, staff invitation resend notices, staff invitation revocation notices, payment validation notices, receipt availability notices, duplicate payment reference alerts, new payment pending validation notices, paid invoice awaiting receipt notices, payment dispute opened notices, refund approval requested notices, invoice void approval requested notices, cash-up submitted for review notices, Branch cash-up discrepancy alerts, commission liability generated notices, merchant suspension warnings, platform fee overdue warnings, financial period ready-for-lock notices, and other service communications.\n\n19.3. Email is a required launch channel. SMS, WhatsApp, or other channels may be enabled later or by configuration. Where such channels are enabled, Personal Data may be Processed through relevant communication service providers.\n\n19.4. Transactional, security, legal, administrative, finance, and service-critical communications may be sent even where a user has opted out of marketing communications, to the extent permitted by Applicable Law.\n\n19.5. Citrus Labs Limited may send marketing or product communications where lawful, subject to applicable consent, opt-out, unsubscribe, or objection rights.\n\n---\n\n## 20. COOKIES, LOCAL STORAGE, SDKs, AND TRACKING TECHNOLOGIES\n\n20.1. Servana may use cookies, pixels, SDKs, local storage, browser storage, session storage, tags, analytics technologies, security cookies, preference technologies, session cookies, device identifiers, and similar technologies.\n\n20.2. Such technologies may be used to authenticate users, maintain sessions, support Magic Link flows, remember preferences, persist theme choices, secure accounts, detect abuse, prevent fraud, measure product usage, troubleshoot errors, improve performance, analyse navigation, support analytics, and provide lawful communications.\n\n20.3. Users may control certain cookies through browser settings, consent tools, or platform preferences where available. Blocking cookies or browser storage may impair authentication, session persistence, account security, finance dashboard functionality, invoice screens, payment validation screens, receipt workflows, cash-up workflows, dispute workflows, export workflows, preference storage, report filters, and other Servana features.\n\n20.4. Citrus Labs Limited will apply consent, notice, or opt-out mechanisms for non-essential cookies or tracking technologies where required by Applicable Law.\n\n---\n\n## 21. ANALYTICS, DIAGNOSTICS, TELEMETRY, AND SERVICE IMPROVEMENT\n\n21.1. Citrus Labs Limited may Process Usage Data, Device Data, diagnostics, product telemetry, performance data, service logs, error reports, finance metadata, validation workflow data, payment-reference error rates, export activity, report activity, dashboard activity, and aggregated statistics to operate, secure, improve, troubleshoot, and scale Servana.\n\n21.2. Citrus Labs Limited may use analytics to understand dashboard performance, feature adoption, system errors, slow endpoints, failed jobs, report generation, email delivery, notification reliability, queue processing, finance export use, validation workflow performance, receipt workflow performance, user onboarding, and general service performance.\n\n21.3. Citrus Labs Limited may create aggregated, anonymised, statistical, or de-identified data from Servana records for internal analytics, product development, commercial planning, security, benchmarking, risk assessment, support prioritisation, and performance monitoring, provided such Processing complies with Applicable Law.\n\n---\n\n## 22. AUTOMATION, RULES ENGINES, DECISION SUPPORT, AND PROFILING\n\n22.1. Servana may use automated rules, calculations, status logic, notifications, alerts, dashboards, workflow controls, and decision-support mechanisms to support platform operations.\n\n22.2. Such mechanisms may include Magic Link expiry, tenant access checks, Branch access checks, duplicate-client prevention, duplicate payment-reference detection, payment validation status logic, partial payment balance calculation, split-payment leg status logic, receipt generation locks, invoice paid status calculation, commission calculation, refund impact calculation, financial period lock logic, cash-up discrepancy alerts, platform fee calculations, platform fee overdue indicators, preferred personnel fee calculations, service fee tier invoice pricing, suspension triggers, suspicious usage alerts, unauthorised access attempt logging, audit severity classifications, and flagged event statuses.\n\n22.3. Servana does not, by this Policy alone, represent that it makes solely automated decisions producing legal or similarly significant effects on Data Subjects without human involvement. Where Applicable Law grants rights relating to automated decision-making or profiling, Citrus Labs Limited will comply to the extent required.\n\n22.4. Automated indicators, risk alerts, duplicate-reference alerts, overdue warnings, validation state changes, receipt readiness states, cash-up discrepancy alerts, no-permission states, suspended Branch states, and suspicious usage flags are support tools and must be interpreted in context by authorised persons where human review is required by Applicable Law, contract, or internal policy.\n\n---\n\n## 23. DATA SHARING AND DISCLOSURE\n\n23.1. Citrus Labs Limited may disclose Personal Data to the following categories of recipients where lawful and necessary:\n\n23.1.1. authorised Citrus Labs Limited personnel, Super Administrators, support personnel, engineers, security personnel, finance personnel, legal personnel, auditors, contractors, and other authorised representatives subject to confidentiality and access controls;\n\n23.1.2. the Customer, Merchant Administrator, authorised merchant users, Branch users, HR users, finance users, front-office users, personnel users, audit users, and invited users according to Customer configuration, tenant scope, Branch scope, role permissions, and Applicable Law;\n\n23.1.3. hosting providers, cloud infrastructure providers, database providers, object storage providers, content delivery providers, monitoring providers, email delivery providers, queue service providers, analytics providers, error tracking providers, security providers, support vendors, and other technical service providers;\n\n23.1.4. payment processors, billing providers, banks, finance partners, or payment service providers where platform fee payment, subscription billing, account-opening fee tracking, statements, or other payment-related functions are enabled;\n\n23.1.5. integration partners, API recipients, webhooks, customer-selected tools, third-party applications, external services, app stores, or embedded services where enabled by the Customer or user;\n\n23.1.6. professional advisers, lawyers, auditors, accountants, consultants, insurers, risk advisers, and business advisers;\n\n23.1.7. regulators, data protection authorities, tax authorities, courts, law enforcement, public authorities, and government bodies where required or permitted by law;\n\n23.1.8. counterparties, advisers, investors, lenders, acquirers, successors, administrators, liquidators, or transferees in connection with mergers, acquisitions, financing, restructuring, insolvency, asset sales, due diligence, or similar transactions;\n\n23.1.9. third parties where disclosure is necessary to protect rights, safety, security, property, users, Customers, Citrus Labs Limited, Servana, or the public.\n\n23.2. Citrus Labs Limited does not permit one merchant to access another merchant’s tenant data except through lawful, controlled, authorised Citrus Labs Limited workflows.\n\n23.3. Citrus Labs Limited may disclose Personal Data where instructed by the Customer, configured by the Customer, necessary to provide Servana, necessary to comply with law, necessary to enforce rights, necessary to resolve disputes, or necessary to protect security and integrity.\n\n---\n\n## 24. NO SALE OF PERSONAL DATA\n\n24.1. Citrus Labs Limited does not sell Personal Data in the ordinary sense of exchanging identifiable Personal Data for monetary consideration as a data broker.\n\n24.2. This clause does not restrict Citrus Labs Limited from using service providers, appointing Subprocessors, conducting analytics, creating aggregated or de-identified information, disclosing Personal Data in a corporate transaction, providing Servana, processing subscriptions, supporting Customers, enforcing agreements, complying with law, securing the platform, or making other lawful disclosures permitted by Applicable Law.\n\n---\n\n## 25. SUBPROCESSORS AND SERVICE PROVIDERS\n\n25.1. Citrus Labs Limited may appoint Subprocessors and service providers to support hosting, storage, authentication, email delivery, notifications, analytics, monitoring, security, backups, support, billing, reporting, exports, infrastructure, development, deployment, maintenance, and other operational functions.\n\n25.2. Citrus Labs Limited will use reasonable contractual, organisational, and technical safeguards with service providers and Subprocessors, including confidentiality obligations, access restrictions, security obligations, lawful Processing limitations, and appropriate contractual protections where required by Applicable Law.\n\n25.3. Citrus Labs Limited may change Subprocessors and service providers where necessary for operational, technical, legal, security, commercial, availability, or business continuity reasons, subject to any notice, objection, or approval rights expressly required by an applicable data processing agreement or Applicable Law.\n\n---\n\n## 26. CROSS-BORDER TRANSFERS\n\n26.1. Personal Data may be stored, hosted, accessed, supported, backed up, transmitted, or otherwise Processed in Kenya, elsewhere in Africa, or outside Africa depending on hosting, infrastructure, support, vendor, backup, security, corporate, and technical requirements.\n\n26.2. Cross-border transfers may occur where Citrus Labs Limited uses cloud hosting, object storage, email providers, support tools, monitoring tools, analytics tools, security services, communication providers, professional advisers, affiliates, contractors, or other vendors located outside the jurisdiction where the Data Subject resides.\n\n26.3. Citrus Labs Limited will implement appropriate safeguards for cross-border transfers where required by Applicable Law, which may include contractual safeguards, data processing agreements, transfer assessments, consent where valid, regulatory approvals where required, or other lawful transfer mechanisms.\n\n26.4. Where a Customer requires specific data localisation, data residency, or jurisdiction-specific storage arrangements, such requirements must be expressly agreed in writing. Citrus Labs Limited does not guarantee jurisdiction-specific storage unless expressly agreed or required by Applicable Law.\n\n---\n\n## 27. DATA LOCALISATION AND JURISDICTION-SPECIFIC STORAGE REQUIREMENTS\n\n27.1. Certain jurisdictions may impose sector-specific, category-specific, or regulator-specific storage, localisation, access, transfer, or recordkeeping requirements.\n\n27.2. Citrus Labs Limited will comply with applicable data localisation or storage requirements to the extent such requirements apply to Citrus Labs Limited, Servana, the relevant Customer, the relevant Data Subject, or the relevant Processing activity.\n\n27.3. The Customer must inform Citrus Labs Limited in writing where the Customer’s use case is subject to special storage, localisation, sectoral, tax, accounting, financial-record, employment-record, consumer-record, or jurisdiction-specific requirements not reasonably apparent from ordinary use of Servana.\n\n---\n\n## 28. DATA RETENTION\n\n28.1. Citrus Labs Limited retains Personal Data for as long as reasonably necessary to provide Servana, administer accounts, support Customers, secure the platform, maintain audit trails, comply with law, enforce rights, resolve disputes, preserve evidence, satisfy tax or accounting obligations, manage backups, maintain business continuity, and fulfil the purposes described in this Policy.\n\n28.2. Retention periods may differ depending on data type, role, Customer configuration, account status, subscription status, payment status, Branch status, dispute status, legal requirements, operational necessity, backup cycles, and contractual obligations.\n\n28.3. Audit logs may be retained for extended periods because they are necessary for security, accountability, dispute resolution, compliance, legal claims, tenant isolation, Branch isolation, financial integrity, evidentiary integrity, suspicious activity investigation, and platform governance.\n\n28.4. Historical records, financial records, invoices, receipts, payment records, payment references, validation records, rejection records, dispute records, external refund records, platform fee records, Branch records, cash-up records, reconciliation records, commission records, finance export records, audit logs, and legally necessary accounting records may be preserved or archived and must not be silently destroyed merely because an account is inactive, suspended, deleted, or terminated.\n\n28.5. Merchant Administrator accounts may be subject to inactivity suspension and deletion rules. Where related Branch accounts or staff accounts are restricted or deleted, historical records, audit logs, financial records, invoices, receipts, payment records, Branch records, cash-up records, reconciliation records, and legally necessary accounting records may continue to be retained or archived where permitted or required.\n\n28.6. Backups, disaster recovery copies, archival records, immutable logs, and technical residual copies may persist for limited periods after deletion from active systems and may be deleted according to backup rotation, legal hold, archival, or disaster recovery procedures.\n\n28.7. Citrus Labs Limited may retain anonymised, aggregated, or de-identified data indefinitely where permitted by Applicable Law.\n\n---\n\n## 29. DELETION, ACCOUNT CLOSURE, SUSPENSION, TERMINATION, AND POST-TERMINATION HANDLING\n\n29.1. Citrus Labs Limited may suspend, restrict, deactivate, or terminate accounts where permitted or required by customer agreements, Applicable Law, security requirements, billing enforcement, abuse controls, inactivity rules, platform-policy enforcement, or internal authorisation controls.\n\n29.2. Suspension or deactivation of merchant staff users, including Merchant Finance users, may invalidate active sessions, invalidate unused Magic Links, block login, preserve historical operational records, and preserve audit logs.\n\n29.3. Merchant Branch accounts may be suspended because of billing issues, abuse, platform-policy enforcement, or Merchant Administrator action. Historical records may remain visible, preserved, archived, or restricted as applicable.\n\n29.4. A Branch must not be closed or archived while live operational records exist, including active queue entries, in-progress service sessions, unpaid invoices, pending payment validations, unissued receipts for validated payments, pending appointment check-ins, unclosed Branch days, or unresolved cash-up discrepancies.\n\n29.5. Termination of a Customer account may result in restriction of active access, export opportunities where contractually available, deletion or archival of Customer Data, continued retention where legally or operationally necessary, and preservation of audit logs, billing records, security logs, financial records, and dispute records.\n\n29.6. Deletion requests are subject to lawful retention, audit preservation, security, legal claims, tax, accounting, backup, Customer-controlled data limitations, financial integrity, receipt integrity, invoice preservation, and evidentiary requirements.\n\n---\n\n## 30. SECURITY MEASURES\n\n30.1. Citrus Labs Limited uses administrative, technical, organisational, and physical safeguards designed to protect Personal Data against unauthorised access, loss, misuse, alteration, disclosure, destruction, or compromise.\n\n30.2. Safeguards may include secure authentication, Magic Link controls, one-time-use tokens, token expiry, session timeout, rate limiting, role-based access control, permission-based access control, tenant-based access control, Branch-based access control, access logging, security monitoring, audit logs, least privilege, optional MFA for high-privilege roles where enabled, signed URLs for private downloads, encryption where appropriate, HTTPS in production, CSRF protection, strict CORS rules, secure cookie configuration, validation of incoming requests, file upload controls, private file storage, secrets management, dependency vulnerability scanning, backups, monitoring, alerting, and incident response procedures.\n\n30.3. Servana’s technical design may include server-side authorisation, Laravel-supported authentication mechanisms, Laravel Policies or Gates, permission packages, validation controls, guarded or fillable model rules, database indexes, tenant ownership keys, public-safe identifiers, database transactions, pagination, background queues, structured logs, performance monitoring, failed job tracking, queue monitoring, production health checks, and vulnerability scanning.\n\n30.4. Audit records may include actor, account or tenant, action, target resource, timestamp, IP address where appropriate, user agent where appropriate, and before-and-after values only where safe and necessary. Sensitive changes may include mandatory before-and-after values, and audit records may use hash or chained-hash controls for tamper detection.\n\n30.5. Frontend permission checks may improve user experience but must not be treated as security controls. Backend, API, form submission, export, download, billing, account settings, invitation, removal, admin, Branch, queue, appointment, service catalogue, finance, invoice, payment validation, receipt, refund, dispute, cash-up, reconciliation, commission, platform fee, and audit screens must be authorised server-side.\n\n30.6. No electronic system, internet transmission, cloud environment, authentication process, storage mechanism, or SaaS platform can be guaranteed absolutely secure. Citrus Labs Limited does not warrant that unauthorised access, cyberattack, data loss, misuse, or security incident can never occur.\n\n---\n\n## 31. MERCHANT FINANCE USER RESPONSIBILITIES\n\n31.1. The Merchant Finance user is responsible for protecting Magic Links, email accounts, devices, sessions, authentication factors, account access, downloaded reports, finance exports, signed URLs, receipts, and finance permissions.\n\n31.2. The Merchant Finance user must use Servana only for lawful finance management, invoice review, payment validation, receipt control, cash-up review, reconciliation, dispute review, external refund recording, commission review where permitted, platform fee review where permitted, finance reporting, finance export where permitted, and other permitted Branch-scoped finance purposes.\n\n31.3. The Merchant Finance user must not access records outside the assigned Branch scope, validate false payments, approve unverifiable payment records, reject payment records without basis, manipulate payment references, override duplicate-reference warnings without authority and reason, generate receipts before payment validation, delete receipts, suppress disputes, record false external refunds, manipulate commission liabilities, misuse platform fee visibility, bypass Branch scope, access another merchant tenant, or access records outside the permitted Customer tenant and Branch scope.\n\n31.4. The Merchant Finance user must not misuse finance visibility, copy data without authority, export data without authority, disclose finance records unlawfully, use client contact details for non-finance purposes, attempt to unmask restricted data unlawfully, circumvent role controls, share Magic Links, or use Servana in violation of Applicable Law.\n\n31.5. The Merchant Finance user must ensure that validation notes, rejection reasons, correction requests, dispute reasons, resolution notes, refund reasons, cash-up discrepancy notes, finance export reasons, support notes, and report notes are accurate, proportionate, professional, necessary, and limited to relevant finance facts.\n\n31.6. The Merchant Finance user must promptly notify Citrus Labs Limited or the relevant Customer administrator of suspected unauthorised access, account compromise, mistaken disclosure, inaccurate Finance Data, suspicious payment records, duplicate reference issues, platform misuse, security vulnerability, unlawful Processing, or any incident that may affect Personal Data.\n\n---\n\n## 32. CUSTOMER RESPONSIBILITIES\n\n32.1. The Customer is responsible for determining the lawfulness of its Processing activities in Servana, including lawful basis, notices, consents, employee and contractor disclosures, Client disclosures, Branch policies, staff permissions, service-recipient communications, payment recordkeeping, export controls, role assignments, finance access, and retention requirements.\n\n32.2. The Customer must provide appropriate privacy notices to its Clients, staff, personnel, employees, contractors, Branch users, finance users, front-office users, audit users, and other Data Subjects where required by Applicable Law.\n\n32.3. The Customer is responsible for handling Data Subject requests relating to Customer-controlled data, unless Applicable Law or a written agreement requires otherwise.\n\n32.4. The Customer must configure Servana in a lawful manner, including role-based access, Branch scope, finance permissions, audit visibility, contact access, export restrictions, staff availability, service eligibility, validation workflows, receipt permissions, dispute workflows, cash-up workflows, platform fee visibility, commission visibility, and notification settings.\n\n32.5. The Customer must not configure or use Servana to unlawfully expose Personal Data, bypass role restrictions, grant excessive finance permissions, export client contact data without lawful authority, record inaccurate payment references knowingly, validate false payment records, issue unsupported receipts, suppress consent records, delete required financial records, alter audit records, or violate Applicable Law.\n\n32.6. The Customer is responsible for supervising Merchant Finance users and ensuring that finance access is limited to appropriate personnel with a legitimate business need and lawful basis.\n\n---\n\n## 33. DATA SUBJECT RIGHTS\n\n33.1. Subject to Applicable Law, lawful exceptions, verification requirements, Customer-control limitations, and contractual obligations, Data Subjects may have rights to request access, correction, rectification, deletion, erasure, restriction, objection, portability, withdrawal of consent, objection to direct marketing, review of certain automated decisions, or other rights available under Applicable Law. Kenya’s ODPC describes Data Subject rights as including rights to be informed of Personal Data use, to access Personal Data in the custody of a controller or processor, to object to Processing, to correction of false or misleading data, and to deletion of false or misleading data. ([ODPC][2])\n\n33.2. Rights are not absolute. Citrus Labs Limited may refuse, limit, delay, or condition a request where permitted or required by law, including where necessary for security, fraud prevention, legal claims, evidentiary preservation, tax or accounting obligations, audit logs, contractual performance, Customer instructions, financial integrity, invoice preservation, receipt preservation, competing rights, excessive requests, manifestly unfounded requests, or legal prohibitions.\n\n33.3. Where a request relates to Customer-controlled data, Citrus Labs Limited may direct the Data Subject to the relevant Customer or Merchant Administrator, assist the Customer in responding, or respond directly where permitted or required by Applicable Law.\n\n33.4. Requests may be submitted to [support@citruslabs.co.ke](mailto:support@citruslabs.co.ke). Citrus Labs Limited may require identity verification, authority verification for agents, account confirmation, request clarification, and information sufficient to locate the relevant data.\n\n---\n\n## 34. PROCEDURE FOR EXERCISING RIGHTS\n\n34.1. A request should identify the Data Subject, the relevant account, merchant tenant, Branch, role, record type, requested right, and sufficient information to enable Citrus Labs Limited to assess and respond.\n\n34.2. Citrus Labs Limited may verify identity through account credentials, email verification, support verification, documentation, authorised-agent confirmation, or other reasonable methods.\n\n34.3. Citrus Labs Limited will respond within timelines required by Applicable Law, subject to lawful extensions, request complexity, request volume, Customer involvement, verification delays, legal restrictions, and technical feasibility.\n\n34.4. Citrus Labs Limited may charge a lawful fee or refuse repetitive, excessive, abusive, unfounded, or unlawful requests where permitted by Applicable Law.\n\n34.5. Where a request seeks deletion, alteration, or suppression of invoices, receipts, payment records, validation records, rejection records, payment references, cash-up records, reconciliation records, refund records, dispute records, commission records, platform fee records, or audit records, Citrus Labs Limited may refuse or limit the request where retention is necessary or permitted for security, fraud prevention, audit integrity, legal claims, regulatory compliance, tax, accounting, dispute resolution, evidentiary preservation, Customer-controlled recordkeeping, receipt integrity, invoice integrity, or financial integrity.\n\n---\n\n## 35. REQUESTS WHERE CITRUS LABS LIMITED ACTS AS PROCESSOR\n\n35.1. Where Citrus Labs Limited acts as Processor for Customer-controlled Personal Data, Citrus Labs Limited may refer Data Subject requests to the Customer, notify the Customer, or assist the Customer in fulfilling the request, subject to the applicable data processing agreement and Applicable Law.\n\n35.2. Citrus Labs Limited may not be able to fulfil a request directly where the Customer determines the relevant Processing purpose and means, where Citrus Labs Limited lacks sufficient authority, where direct fulfilment would conflict with Customer instructions, or where direct fulfilment would impair another Data Subject’s rights or legal obligations.\n\n35.3. The Customer, Merchant Administrator, and Merchant Finance user must cooperate with Citrus Labs Limited where necessary to respond to Data Subject requests, regulatory requests, security incidents, legal claims, or lawful investigations involving Customer-controlled data.\n\n---\n\n## 36. COMPLAINTS AND REGULATORY CONTACTS\n\n36.1. Privacy complaints, questions, or requests may be directed to Citrus Labs Limited at [support@citruslabs.co.ke](mailto:support@citruslabs.co.ke).\n\n36.2. Where Applicable Law provides a right to complain to a competent data protection authority, a Data Subject may lodge a complaint with the relevant authority. In Kenya, the competent authority may include the Office of the Data Protection Commissioner where applicable. The ODPC states that its functions include regulating Personal Data Processing, protecting privacy of individuals, establishing legal and institutional mechanisms to protect Personal Data, and providing Data Subjects with rights and remedies. ([ODPC][3])\n\n36.3. Citrus Labs Limited may cooperate with competent authorities to the extent required by Applicable Law and may require Customers, Merchant Administrators, and Merchant Finance users to cooperate where the complaint concerns Customer-controlled data, Finance Data, Branch records, access logs, security events, or Customer tenant activity.\n\n---\n\n## 37. DATA BREACHES AND SECURITY INCIDENTS\n\n37.1. Citrus Labs Limited maintains procedures to identify, investigate, mitigate, document, and respond to security incidents and Personal Data breaches.\n\n37.2. Where Citrus Labs Limited determines that notification is required by Applicable Law, Citrus Labs Limited will notify affected Customers, Data Subjects, regulators, or other parties as legally required, taking into account the nature of the incident, Personal Data involved, likely consequences, mitigation measures, legal instructions, and ongoing investigations.\n\n37.3. Where Citrus Labs Limited acts as Processor, Citrus Labs Limited will notify the relevant Customer of a Personal Data breach in accordance with the applicable data processing agreement and Applicable Law.\n\n37.4. The Merchant Finance user must cooperate with Citrus Labs Limited and the Customer in incident investigation, containment, mitigation, communication, evidence preservation, credential resets, access review, finance review, invoice review, payment record review, receipt review, refund review, cash-up review, export review, and corrective action where the incident relates to the Customer’s tenant, Finance Data, Branch records, users, devices, credentials, configurations, finance access, or Customer-controlled data.\n\n37.5. Any notification, cooperation, investigation, mitigation, or remedial step shall not be construed as an admission of fault, liability, causation, negligence, unlawfulness, or damages.\n\n---\n\n## 38. LEGAL PROCESS, LAW ENFORCEMENT, AND REGULATORY REQUESTS\n\n38.1. Citrus Labs Limited may disclose Personal Data where required or permitted by Applicable Law, court order, subpoena, warrant, regulatory demand, law enforcement request, public authority request, tax authority request, national security request, fraud investigation, emergency disclosure, legal claim, or rights enforcement.\n\n38.2. Citrus Labs Limited may challenge, narrow, delay, or refuse requests where legally permissible and appropriate.\n\n38.3. Citrus Labs Limited may notify affected Customers or Data Subjects of legal requests where permitted, unless prohibited by law, court order, security necessity, investigation requirements, or risk of harm.\n\n38.4. Citrus Labs Limited may preserve Personal Data, Finance Data, invoices, receipts, payment records, refund records, dispute records, cash-up records, reconciliation records, commission records, platform fee records, Branch records, audit logs, access logs, and security logs where necessary to comply with legal process, regulatory inquiry, litigation hold, dispute resolution, security investigation, billing dispute, payment dispute, or enforcement of rights.\n\n---\n\n## 39. BUSINESS TRANSFERS\n\n39.1. Personal Data may be disclosed, transferred, assigned, or otherwise Processed in connection with a merger, acquisition, financing, restructuring, insolvency, sale of assets, transfer of business, change of control, due diligence review, investment transaction, or similar event.\n\n39.2. Any recipient of Personal Data in such context shall be subject to appropriate confidentiality, security, and lawful Processing obligations to the extent required by Applicable Law and transaction circumstances.\n\n---\n\n## 40. THIRD-PARTY WEBSITES, INTEGRATIONS, APIs, AND EXTERNAL SERVICES\n\n40.1. Servana may contain links to third-party websites, external services, app stores, embedded services, integration partners, APIs, communication channels, payment or billing services, or Customer-selected tools.\n\n40.2. Citrus Labs Limited is not responsible for the privacy practices, security, content, terms, or Processing activities of third-party services not controlled by Citrus Labs Limited.\n\n40.3. Where a Customer enables an integration, export, webhook, API connection, external communication channel, or third-party tool, the Customer, Merchant Administrator, and authorised users are responsible for ensuring that such integration is lawful, properly configured, secure, and consistent with obligations to Data Subjects.\n\n40.4. Merchant Finance users must not send finance reports, screenshots, logs, evidence, client records, financial records, payment records, invoices, receipts, refund records, dispute records, cash-up records, reconciliation records, commission records, or platform fee records to external services unless authorised by the Customer and permitted by Applicable Law.\n\n---\n\n## 41. OPEN-SOURCE, THIRD-PARTY SOFTWARE, AND INFRASTRUCTURE DEPENDENCIES\n\n41.1. Servana may use open-source software, commercial software, frameworks, libraries, infrastructure components, cloud services, queues, cache systems, databases, object storage, search systems, monitoring tools, and development dependencies.\n\n41.2. Citrus Labs Limited may Process Personal Data through such dependencies only to the extent necessary for Servana’s operation, security, maintenance, hosting, storage, monitoring, support, deployment, and development.\n\n41.3. Citrus Labs Limited does not disclose confidential internal architecture beyond what is necessary in this Policy and applicable agreements.\n\n---\n\n## 42. PAYMENT AND BILLING DATA\n\n42.1. Servana records offline merchant-client payment information. Supported offline payment methods may include cash, M-Pesa, bank transfer, card terminal, voucher, split payment, and other merchant-defined offline payment methods.\n\n42.2. Servana may record payment method, payment amount, payment reference, payment date and time, payment note, recorded-by user, validated-by user, validation status, validation note, rejection reason, Branch identifier, merchant identifier, invoice identifier, payment record identifier, receipt identifier, duplicate reference status, dispute status, external refund status, and cash-up reconciliation data.\n\n42.3. Merchant Finance users may view, validate, reject, correct, dispute, export, or otherwise Process Branch payment records, payment validation status, pending validation lists, receipt status, payment method breakdowns, daily Branch payment summaries, expected versus recorded amounts, discrepancy notes, refund records, dispute records, and cash-up review records where permitted. Sensitive payment data may be masked unless permission allows full visibility.\n\n42.4. Servana does not, by this Policy alone, directly process client-to-merchant payments inside the platform. Where payment processing is enabled through a third-party payment processor or future feature, additional payment terms, processor notices, and lawful disclosures may apply.\n\n42.5. Citrus Labs Limited may Process platform fee records, subscription records, merchant balance records, account-opening fee records where applicable, service fee tier settings, billing cycle records, overdue status records, suspension triggers, fee exemption records, platform fee disputes, merchant-facing statements, and related ledgers.\n\n42.6. The Merchant Finance user acknowledges that the recording, validation, rejection, dispute, refund, export, reconciliation, and receipt functions in Servana may affect Customer reports, Client-facing receipts, commission visibility, platform fee reconciliation, dispute records, and legally relevant financial records.\n\n---\n\n## 43. SUPPORT, TROUBLESHOOTING, DIAGNOSTICS, AND REMOTE ASSISTANCE\n\n43.1. Citrus Labs Limited may Process Personal Data in support interactions, troubleshooting, diagnostics, bug reports, screenshots, attachments, logs, API traces, export issues, invoice issues, payment validation issues, receipt issues, refund issues, dispute issues, cash-up issues, reconciliation issues, platform fee issues, commission issues, audit issues, account access issues, and other support matters.\n\n43.2. Support access may be restricted, logged, audited, time-bound, and limited to the information reasonably necessary to resolve the support matter, investigate abuse, secure the platform, comply with law, or enforce rights.\n\n43.3. Users must not submit unnecessary Sensitive Personal Data in support requests, attachments, screenshots, or diagnostic materials.\n\n43.4. Merchant Finance users must ensure that any screenshots, log excerpts, reports, or attachments submitted for support do not unnecessarily disclose client contact details, payment-sensitive details, staff records, minors’ data, payment references, financial reports, or other restricted information.\n\n---\n\n## 44. BACKUPS, DISASTER RECOVERY, AND BUSINESS CONTINUITY\n\n44.1. Citrus Labs Limited may create and maintain backups, disaster recovery copies, redundancy systems, archival records, restoration points, logs, and business continuity procedures.\n\n44.2. Backup data may include Personal Data and may persist after deletion from active systems until the relevant backup cycle expires or the data is overwritten, unless legal hold, security investigation, regulatory obligations, tax or accounting obligations, audit preservation, or dispute preservation require longer retention.\n\n44.3. Restored data may reintroduce Personal Data into active systems where necessary for disaster recovery, incident response, legal preservation, business continuity, financial record restoration, audit restoration, or service restoration.\n\n---\n\n## 45. AGGREGATED, ANONYMISED, AND DE-IDENTIFIED DATA\n\n45.1. Citrus Labs Limited may use aggregated, anonymised, or de-identified data for analytics, reporting, product improvement, security, benchmarking, commercial planning, research, capacity planning, fraud prevention, finance workflow assessment, platform performance assessment, and risk analysis.\n\n45.2. Citrus Labs Limited may retain and use such data after account closure, termination, deletion, expiry, or discontinuance of access to Servana, provided such use does not unlawfully identify a Data Subject.\n\n---\n\n## 46. INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY AND DATA OWNERSHIP BOUNDARIES\n\n46.1. Privacy rights are distinct from intellectual property rights, contractual rights, database rights, platform ownership, software rights, analytics rights, and commercial rights.\n\n46.2. Customers and Data Subjects may retain rights in their Personal Data as provided by Applicable Law and applicable agreements. Citrus Labs Limited retains ownership of Servana, its software, interfaces, configurations, workflows, templates, documentation, system designs, platform metadata, aggregated data, de-identified insights, security telemetry, audit structures, improvements, and intellectual property, subject to applicable agreements and law.\n\n46.3. Nothing in this Policy transfers ownership of Servana or Citrus Labs Limited intellectual property to any Merchant Finance user, Customer, User, Client, or Data Subject.\n\n---\n\n## 47. CONFIDENTIALITY AND PROTECTED BUSINESS INFORMATION\n\n47.1. Servana may Process confidential Customer business information, financial records, staff records, Branch performance records, platform fee records, pricing records, service catalogues, business operations, reports, disputes, cash-up records, reconciliation records, commission records, audit logs, export records, and operational records.\n\n47.2. Citrus Labs Limited applies confidentiality obligations and access controls to protect confidential information, subject to lawful disclosure, Customer configuration, support access, legal process, professional adviser access, security needs, and applicable agreements.\n\n47.3. The Merchant Finance user must treat client records, staff records, Branch records, financial records, payment records, payment references, invoices, receipts, refund records, dispute records, cash-up records, reconciliation records, commission records, platform fee records, security records, audit records, reports, screenshots, exports, and support records as confidential and must not disclose them except as authorised by the Customer, Citrus Labs Limited, Applicable Law, or relevant agreements.\n\n---\n\n## 48. DIRECT MARKETING AND OPT-OUT RIGHTS\n\n48.1. Citrus Labs Limited may send lawful marketing, product updates, surveys, event notices, service announcements, or commercial communications to business contacts where permitted by Applicable Law.\n\n48.2. Recipients may opt out of marketing communications through available unsubscribe mechanisms or by contacting [support@citruslabs.co.ke](mailto:support@citruslabs.co.ke).\n\n48.3. Opting out of marketing does not prevent service, legal, security, billing, transactional, finance, operational, or administrative communications.\n\n---\n\n## 49. CHANGES TO THIS POLICY\n\n49.1. Citrus Labs Limited may amend, update, replace, or revise this Policy from time to time to reflect changes in Servana, legal requirements, operational practices, security measures, products, features, vendors, integrations, business structure, or regulatory expectations.\n\n49.2. Citrus Labs Limited may provide notice of material changes through the website, dashboard, account portal, email, in-product notice, legal page, customer communication, or other reasonable method.\n\n49.3. Continued access to or use of Servana after the effective date of an updated Policy constitutes acknowledgement of the updated Policy, subject to Applicable Law and any rights expressly available to the Data Subject.\n\n49.4. Where Applicable Law requires consent for a specific new Processing activity, Citrus Labs Limited will seek such consent where required.\n\n---\n\n## 50. GOVERNING LAW AND INTERPRETIVE PROVISIONS\n\n50.1. This Policy is primarily anchored in Kenya because Citrus Labs Limited is located in Nairobi, Kenya, and Servana is owned and operated by Citrus Labs Limited.\n\n50.2. This Policy shall be interpreted consistently with Applicable Law, including applicable privacy and data protection laws in jurisdictions where Servana lawfully operates or where Data Subjects are protected.\n\n50.3. Nothing in this Policy limits non-waivable rights available to Data Subjects under Applicable Law.\n\n50.4. This Policy does not create warranties, service levels, payment obligations, acceptable use rules, accounting advice, tax advice, refund promises, payment-processing obligations, or liability limitations except to the extent privacy-related clauses necessarily cross-reference those matters. Such matters are governed by separate applicable terms and agreements.\n\n---\n\n## 51. CONTACT INFORMATION\n\n51.1. Privacy inquiries, Data Subject requests, complaints, security concerns, and data protection communications may be directed to:\n\n**Citrus Labs Limited**\n**P.O. Box 23983 - 00100, Nairobi, Kenya**\n**Email:** [support@citruslabs.co.ke](mailto:support@citruslabs.co.ke)\n\n51.2. Requests should include sufficient information to identify the requester, relevant account, relevant merchant tenant, relevant Branch, relevant role, requested action, and affected Personal Data.\n\n---\n\n## 52. FINAL LEGAL CLAUSES\n\n52.1. If any provision of this Policy is held to be invalid, unlawful, or unenforceable, the remaining provisions shall remain valid and enforceable to the maximum extent permitted by Applicable Law.\n\n52.2. Provisions relating to retention, audit logs, security, confidentiality, legal claims, regulatory cooperation, business transfers, aggregated data, de-identified data, intellectual property, interpretation, and evidentiary effect shall survive account closure, termination, suspension, deletion, expiry, or discontinuance of access to Servana.\n\n52.3. Failure by Citrus Labs Limited to enforce any provision of this Policy shall not constitute a waiver of that provision or any other right.\n\n52.4. This Policy may be published electronically, accepted electronically, incorporated by reference, displayed in-product, linked through account flows, and retained as an electronic record. Electronic versions, logs, timestamps, acceptance records, publication records, account records, finance records, invoice records, receipt records, payment records, dispute records, refund records, cash-up records, reconciliation records, export records, and audit records may be used as evidence to the fullest extent permitted by Applicable Law.\n\n52.5. The English-language version of this Policy governs unless Citrus Labs Limited expressly publishes a legally controlling translated version. Translations, where provided, are for convenience unless expressly stated otherwise.\n\n52.6. Headings are for convenience only and do not limit the scope or interpretation of any clause.\n\n52.7. In the event of conflict between this Policy and a more specific signed agreement governing the same Processing activity, the more specific agreement shall control for that Processing activity, unless Applicable Law requires otherwise.\n\n\n";

